compiler-performance-followups -- optional performance work left when the compiler epic closed
=============================================================================================

Status: DEFERRED
Priority: P3
From: issues/closed/compiler.issue (closed 2026-10-06), its "STILL OPEN after
slice 27" list. None of these is a defect; each is a measured opportunity or
a design choice to revisit with a measurement in hand.

- A host-independent .lap. Deferred on purpose (closed
  clal-compiled-artefact.issue): a .lap is the host's own FASL, so an
  SBCL-built one does not load in CCL nor across clautolisp versions. CLISP
  is the existence proof that a portable format is possible and the reason it
  costs speed.
- What a call still allocates (after slice 17): a dynamic-frame struct and a
  dynamic-binding struct per parameter, the &rest list and the three-cons
  call-stack entry in CALL-AUTOLISP-FUNCTION-IN-CONTEXT. Pooling needs the
  ownership rules restated first (a closure captures its environment; the
  debugger's snapshot reaches frames), as for the shadow-frame pool of
  poll-point-restart-cost.
- Reading the optimisation qualities from the configuration module, so a
  project sets them once (the --optimize half is done, slice 11); belongs with
  the "gather options / getenv / defaults into the configuration module" work.
- A lambda in an INTERPRETED loop mints closures no site can share (the site
  covers the compiled path only). Costs nothing today: the loop itself is
  interpreted.
- Poll points: what remains is the per-poll-point work the debugger needs
  (two POLL-POINT calls, enter/exit bookkeeping, UNWIND-PROTECT).
- Open-coding has reached diminishing returns for the operators; LENGTH, NTH,
  MEMBER, ASSOC are the wrong shape for it (recorded so nobody adds them on
  the grounds that they are frequent).

Reopen an item with a measurement showing it matters.
